{{!-- lab-particle-backdrop-wrap: gives #201D23 its own layer BETWEEN
      gradflow-page-bg (z-index:0) and the particle canvas (z-index:1), so
      the requested stack — page-bg lowest, #201D23 backdrop, particles,
      section content — is actually achievable. This CANNOT live as
      .gradient-frame's own `background` (tried first): that element is
      position:relative;z-index:2 (gradient-frame.css) so its own CONTENT
      canB render above particles, but a CSS stacking context is atomic for
      paint order — EVERY descendant, including a background layer,
      inherits the context's global rank relative to things outside it.
      There is no z-ihndex you can give a child that makes it paint below
      something the PARENT context already ranks above. This plain wrapper
      (position:relative, no z-index — doesn't itself create a stacking
      context) lets the backdrop div and .gradient-frame each rank
      independently against the particle canvas: backdrop at z-index:0
      (ties gradflow-page-bg, wins via later DOM order — same mechanism
      that partial itself uses against #page-backdrop), .gradient-frame
      unchanged at z-index:2. See .lab-particle-backdrop in
      posts-tabs-grid-lab.css for why it's inset from the top/bottom by
      the band height rather than the whole box (must not repaint the
      band's own fade-to-page-bg edge). --}}
      <!--
<div class="lab-particle-backdrop-wrap">
  <div class="lab-particle-backdrop" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  -->

  {{!-- Animated gradient bands above and below the whole Lab section, via
      the reusable gradient-frame component (gradient-frame.js/.css,
      loaded site-wide in default.hbs) — it injects the two in-flow bands
      itself, so this only needs the wrapper.

      Every knob is spelled out below rather than left to the component's
      defaults, so this element is the single place to tune the look.
      Changing one here affects only this section; delete an attribute to
      fall back to the component default (see GRADIENT-FRAME-SYSTEM.md for
      the full list and what each does).

      The island's own palette (identical in both themes) is NOT here: it is
      authored in posts-tabs-grid-lab.css as a :root override of the shift
      tokens — see the "FIXED PALETTE" block there. Colours that must reach
      the fixed nav have to live at :root; only the band's own wave colours
      belong on this element. --}}


  
  <div class="lab-particle-backdrop-wrap">

<div class="lab-particle-backdrop" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<div class="gradient-frame"

data-gradient-frame

{{!-- Outer (page-facing) edge — configurable per instance (see

gradient-frame.css's own doc on this attribute):

"true" — fades to transparent instead of a flat

--color-background-matched color, blending into

whatever's actually behind the frame.

"false" — solid data-gradient-outer color (theme background by

default), a flat hard edge. Currently "false" here,

matching Lab's own reversion (posts-tabs-grid-lab.hbs)

— tried "true" first, reverted per explicit request.

Inner edge (this section's own invert-mode palette) stays fully

opaque either way — this only ever touches the side facing the

page. --}}

data-gradient-fade-outer="true"

{{!-- .posts-tabs-grid-lab-section has no background of its own by

design — the particle canvas shows through its gaps wherever it's

scrolled to. Without this, .gradient-frame's own solid inner fill

(data-gradient-inner below) paints across the WHOLE box at this

element's z-index:2, which sits ABOVE the particle canvas

(z-index:1) — hiding it completely for this section's entire

scroll range, not just behind actual card content. Same fix

already documented/applied to this identical Lab wrapper

elsewhere in this file (see the commented-out reference block

below, data-gradient-transparent-middle="true") — this live

instance was missing it. See gradient-frame.css's own doc on

this attribute for the full stacking-context reasoning. --}}

data-gradient-transparent-middle="false"

data-gradient-inner="#2D104A"

{{!-- BAND HEIGHT (new attribute, 2026-08-10) — the physical height of

the box each wave lives in. Previously only settable as the

--gradient-frame-edge-height CSS custom property, with no data-

equivalent, so it was the one knob on this component that wasn't

where the others are. Default is 480px.

This is a DIFFERENT question from amplitude (how tall the wave

swings inside the box) and wave2-width (how thick the second

wave's band is) — a taller box is what gives the wave room to

read as a long diagonal sweep rather than a compact stripe. --}}

data-gradient-edge-height="700px"

{{!-- BASE WAVE — the 4-colour ramp's animated core. --}}

data-gradient-wave-a="#392341"

data-gradient-wave-b="#19141E"

data-gradient-scale="0.9"

data-gradient-speed="0.7"

  

{{!-- SECOND WAVE — composited in the same shader (no extra canvas).

A DIFFERENT scale is what makes the two cross instead of stack.

Omit wave2-a/-b to reuse the base pair at partial opacity. --}}

data-gradient-wave2-a="#392341"

data-gradient-wave2-b="#19141E"

data-gradient-wave2-opacity="0.75"

data-gradient-wave2-scale="1.9"

data-gradient-wave2-speed="0.45"

data-gradient-wave2-center="0.52"

data-gradient-wave2-width="0.64"

  

{{!-- WAVE HEIGHT + PULSE — all three were UNSET on this instance until

2026-08-09, so they were silently running on gradient-frame.js's

DEFAULTS (amplitude 1, breathe 0.4, breatheRate 0.12). That's why

the height pulse read as barely-there here: it was never actually

configured, just inherited.

amplitude — overall wave HEIGHT multiplier (1 = original).

Distinct from `scale` above, which is FREQUENCY

("squiggliness"), not height.

breathe — depth of the calm height pulse; 0 = flat.

breathe-rate — pulse speed, applied on top of each layer's own

`speed`, so the two waves keep breathing at

different paces instead of in lockstep. --}}

data-gradient-amplitude="1.2"

{{!-- breathe was 5.85 — far past useful. The shader ranges the height

multiplier over 1-breathe .. 1+breathe, so anything above ~1

spends most of the cycle CLAMPED at fully-collapsed, which reads

as a dense flat mass rather than a breathing wave. 0.8 keeps a

strong stretch (20%..180% height) while still moving. --}}

data-gradient-breathe="1.4"

data-gradient-breathe-rate="0.35"

  

{{!-- BASE-WAVE OPACITY (new, 2026-08-09) — wave2 always had its own

opacity; the base ramp had none. Blends the animated ramp back

toward the band's flat vertical gradient (NOT toward transparency,

which would break the band's seamless page-background match).

1 = fully animated, 0 = static gradient. --}}

data-gradient-wave-opacity="0.72"

data-gradient-edge-height="6.95"

{{!-- PARALLAX — viewport-heights of scroll.

The TOP and BOTTOM bands used to share one value, so they drifted

together and never separated. The *-top / *-bottom overrides

(new, 2026-08-09) give each band its own rate; the shared

data-gradient-parallax / -wave2-parallax below stay as the

fallback for anything not overridden. Opposite signs between the

two BANDS is what reads as the two edges of the frame moving

independently as you scroll. --}}

{{!-- These are now TRUE scroll parallax. u_scroll used to be absolute

page scroll (scrollY / innerHeight), so on this ~14-viewport page

a band sitting 4 viewports down received a CONSTANT ~4 before any

scrolling happened — and since waveFlow() multiplies

`u_scroll * parallax` by `taper = sin(uv.y * PI)` (zero at both

band edges), that constant shoved the wave into its own taper and

flattened it, worse the further down the page it sat. Fixed in

gradflow-background.js: u_scroll is now each canvas's OWN position

relative to the viewport centre, sweeping about -0.8 -> +1.2 as

the band crosses the screen (measured), centred near 0.

Because the input is small and centred now, these values can be

real magnitudes rather than the tiny numbers that were previously

needed just to avoid the taper.

Opposite signs between top and bottom is what makes the frame's

two edges drift apart as you scroll. --}}

data-gradient-parallax="0.24"

data-gradient-wave2-parallax="-0.3"

data-gradient-parallax-top="-0.28"

data-gradient-parallax-bottom="0.28"

data-gradient-wave2-parallax-top="0.36"

data-gradient-wave2-parallax-bottom="-0.36"

  

{{!-- PALETTE-SHIFT TIMING — ramp length in viewport heights, and the

band render resolution (see gradflow-background.js). --}}

data-gradient-enter-span="0.5"

data-gradient-exit-span="0.5"

data-gradient-resolution-scale="0.35">

